How to Run(Bash): 

sudo python3 lab6.py http://example.com/

For tester(Bash): 

./test.sh

To capture(Bash):

sudo tcpdump -i any -w test.pcap tcp port 80


Questions: 

1) In lab 4 you sent a GET request using the sockets library. What did the sockets library do for you?

Answer: The sockets handled all TCP connection details therefore I didn't have to manually build packets

2) Before you submit this lab, what program should you use to analyze your pcap? Did the server send the complete HTML?

Answer: Wireshark is for analyzing the pcap whereas the server usually sends only the first packet of the HTML since it stops after one GET request and doesn't handle streaming

3) Is your program guaranteed to receive a complete HTML response? Why or why not?

Answer: No, this is cause scapy only sends a single GET requests and doesn't implement full TCP assembly and or multiple packets of the HTTP

4) Can you merge the final ACK of the three-way handshake with the GET request?

Answer: Yes, you can set both ACK and PSH flags in one packet and include a GET request and makes it all one single part 

5) Can Scapy be used to send other types of packets? Give an example.

Answer: Yes, Scapy can create and send packets at any layer for example ICMP, UDP, and TCP



